A||N||2||ElricSI||Idle and Hum||||9||
Z||000000||ElricSI||02-25-2001||05:38 PM||||Howdy, my first post here, although I have been observing for some time.<P>In my 85 GT four speed, there are two bothersome problems:<P>1. For a long time now there is a loud humming coming from below (or behind) the center console.  It begins after a little while (usually not right away) and does not go away or change at all (until the car is shut off of course.)<P>2. After warming up, the RPM's jump around when idling.  They ramp to 2000 then drop to like 700 where the car almost stalls then start going up again, over and over.  The car also hesitates very bad at low RPM's.  After 2000, maybe 2500 there are never any problems.  The car drives excellent at highway speeds.<P>Thanks in advance for any help!<P>------------------<BR>Proud Owner of 85 GT||||reg||9
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||02-25-2001||07:36 PM||||First one sounds like the fuel pump is going to take a hike for you. Sort of hard to tell with the limited info given. (rolling, not rolling, milage of the car ect.)<P>Second one indicates a bad IAC valve or vacunm leak or EGR valve ect. Any codes?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||02-26-2001||07:42 AM||||My guess is the EGR tube is cracked causing the rpm swing.  They crack with age and when they do the engine sucks in un-metered air.  The ECM compensates due to O2 readings and pours in more gas.<P>New EGR Tubes are a bit expensive.  Paul McKibben has adapted a Pontiac 6000 2.8L one to fit the Fiero and has one on E-bay right now pretty cheap.  I believe the Camaro 2.8L is the same.  It only requires elongating the flange mounting holes on one end of the tube to make it fit a Fiero EGR.<P>The stumbling could be caused by the throttle position sensor (TPS).  This is mounted on the side of the throttle body (rear side) and is connected to the linkage that opens the butterfly.  It is a variable resistor that controls a signal to the ECM telling it how much the throttle is open.  When they get tired they start to lie to the ECM about how far the throttle is open and cause imporper fuel/air ratio for a given throttle opening.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 02-26-2001).]||||reg||1
